WEBVTT

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.480
-We're talking about
normals today.

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.720
Normals. It's a bit of a weird

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:07.040
word because you say day to day.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:08.700
That's normal.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.200
That looks like a normal
looking muffin over there in

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.815
the pastry section
of the coffee shop.

00:00:13.815 --> 00:00:15.315
I was doing some reading.

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:17.655
I'm like, what does normal
normals have to do with the

00:00:17.655 --> 00:00:19.495
stuff we're talking about?

00:00:19.495 --> 00:00:21.655
Apparently, 
it originates from

00:00:21.655 --> 00:00:23.815
the Latin word for a T ruler.

00:00:23.815 --> 00:00:25.830
So a T ruler, 
like the one you bought.

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:27.330
-The one I bought.
-The one you got for T square.

00:00:27.330 --> 00:00:28.830
Yeah, a T square.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.330
Yeah. I have to brush up

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.830
on my Latin.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.330
-So the word normal
means T square?

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:36.230
-Yeah. And it's
signifying 90 degrees.

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:37.810
-Got it.

00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:40.475
-Okay. So the normal, I've got a
shape here, the normal on this

00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:44.235
ball is 90 degrees
to the surface.

00:00:44.235 --> 00:00:47.755
And as you move around the
surface, you always want that

00:00:47.755 --> 00:00:49.255
normal vector.

00:00:49.255 --> 00:00:50.755
It's a direction.

00:00:50.755 --> 00:00:52.255
Normal of this table

00:00:52.255 --> 00:00:53.755
pointing straight up.

00:00:53.755 --> 00:00:55.300
So, yeah, my students get a bit
confused because the word

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.220
normal means not, like,
out of the ordinary.

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:01.300
How do we represent
these in math?

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:02.800
But you know what?

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.740
Before I talk about the math,
maybe we should talk about why

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.735
do we need normals?

00:01:06.735 --> 00:01:08.415
What are normals used for?

00:01:08.415 --> 00:01:10.015
-Don't look at me.

00:01:12.015 --> 00:01:13.855
We don't make them.

00:01:13.855 --> 00:01:16.495
You know, they
come with the models that we

00:01:16.495 --> 00:01:19.010
create, but we can
manipulate them.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.410
So you need to have normals
because you need to know which

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:23.170
way light's bouncing
off the surface.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:25.970
But if we make something flat,
generally all the normals point

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.650
up like your pen does, and
light bounces off it correctly.

00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:32.530
And with the sphere, it looks
like it's a sphere because it's

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:35.135
got normals on it, and light
bounces off it correctly.

00:01:35.135 --> 00:01:36.635
-That's how I use it.

00:01:36.635 --> 00:01:38.135
It's just for lighting.

00:01:38.135 --> 00:01:39.635
-Just for lighting.

00:01:39.635 --> 00:01:41.135
Okay. So that's why
we need normals.

00:01:41.135 --> 00:01:44.655
Mathematically speaking, I
brought a vector with me today.

00:01:44.655 --> 00:01:46.735
I like to do that.

00:01:46.735 --> 00:01:50.335
The kind of confusing thing
about a normal, which is

00:01:50.335 --> 00:01:52.230
direction, is that it's kind of

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:55.190
similar mathematically
to a position.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:58.790
If you have something in the
world that has an x, a y, and a

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:00.550
z position.

00:02:00.550 --> 00:02:02.950
But if you're pointing
somewhere, you could think of

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:04.978
as what I'm pointing at has

00:02:04.978 --> 00:02:07.005
acertain change in x, 
change in

00:02:07.005 --> 00:02:08.525
y, change in z.

00:02:08.525 --> 00:02:10.925
So a lot of times when you're
writing out normals and

00:02:10.925 --> 00:02:14.525
vectors, you could just use the
first three numbers here, but

00:02:14.525 --> 00:02:16.025
then there's the confusion.

00:02:16.025 --> 00:02:19.085
Like, are you talking about
a position or you're talking

00:02:19.085 --> 00:02:21.140
about a direction?

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:24.980
And the way out of this bind
is to have one more number.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:27.660
This is the w component, the

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.340
fourth number for this vector.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.435
So for positions,

00:02:33.435 --> 00:02:37.275
we use a w equal to one.

00:02:37.275 --> 00:02:40.715
And that basically means
that you start at the origin

00:02:40.715 --> 00:02:43.275
and then you move over x y z.

00:02:43.275 --> 00:02:45.115
But for normals, which are

00:02:45.115 --> 00:02:48.400
directions, they're
not positions,

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.040
what we end up doing is

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:53.680
giving it a w of zero.

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:55.880
And the reason zero is the

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:58.080
right number to choose is

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.580
something we'll talk about

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.520
later is the perspective divide.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.180
-Oh, dear.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:06.535
-Okay. So I blame Scott for this
because he makes a matrix that

00:03:06.535 --> 00:03:08.055
messes around with the w.

00:03:08.055 --> 00:03:12.055
We'll find out later that
as this w gets smaller and

00:03:12.055 --> 00:03:15.975
smaller, to get it into the
screen, to get it into three d

00:03:15.975 --> 00:03:19.460
space, you have to divide out
that number, a small number,

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:22.900
divide the x y z by the w.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:26.740
And as w gets smaller and
smaller, you notice if you

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.980
divide by a small number,

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:31.220
the x y z get bigger 
and bigger and

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:33.255
bigger to the limit.

00:03:33.255 --> 00:03:35.655
The limit is if you divide by

00:03:35.655 --> 00:03:37.975
zero, x y z is

00:03:37.975 --> 00:03:40.295
infinitely far away.

00:03:40.295 --> 00:03:43.575
So that's one way to think
about a normal is that it's

00:03:43.575 --> 00:03:45.075
something far away.

00:03:45.075 --> 00:03:47.482
And to demonstrate this, 
maybe

00:03:47.482 --> 00:03:49.890
Paul and I can point at Scott.

00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.210
So Scott isn't
infinitely far away.

00:03:52.210 --> 00:03:54.150
-Not yet. 
-You're not in trouble.

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:55.650
-Okay.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:58.393
-If you look at my hand

00:03:58.393 --> 00:04:01.135
and Paul's hand, 
they're at

00:04:01.135 --> 00:04:04.495
different angles pointing
at the same position.

00:04:04.495 --> 00:04:07.775
But as Scott moves away,
imagine he's, oh yeah, imagine

00:04:07.775 --> 00:04:11.135
he's infinitely far away

00:04:11.135 --> 00:04:15.130
and Paul and I are still
pointing at him, our arms are

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:16.890
going to be parallel.

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:20.170
So the idea is even though Paul
and I have different positions,

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:22.410
when an object is infinite

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:24.490
sorry, you're a
person, not an object.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:25.990
-It's alright.

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:29.805
-When a valued colleague is
infinitely far away, it doesn't

00:04:29.805 --> 00:04:32.925
matter that we've translate
like, my position is different

00:04:32.925 --> 00:04:34.425
to Paul's.

00:04:34.425 --> 00:04:36.445
We have a different
translation, but our direction

00:04:36.445 --> 00:04:38.185
is the same.

00:04:38.185 --> 00:04:40.195
-Now because 
you set the w to

00:04:40.195 --> 00:04:42.205
zero, you're not reaching all

00:04:42.205 --> 00:04:43.705
the way to infinity.

00:04:43.705 --> 00:04:45.205
You're just setting the

00:04:45.205 --> 00:04:46.970
direction to be
towards infinity.

00:04:46.970 --> 00:04:48.470
-Yes. Good point.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:49.970
Yeah. It's a direction.

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:51.470
-Because the normal

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:52.970
has a value or a
length of just one.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:54.470
-They're normalized normals.

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.025
-Normally.

00:04:56.025 --> 00:04:57.785
-This is how we write
it mathematically.

00:04:57.785 --> 00:05:00.425
Now this, I'm blaming Paul for
this because I always think

00:05:00.425 --> 00:05:03.545
of Paul making the
normal in model space.

00:05:03.545 --> 00:05:05.705
But to get to Cassidy's coffee

00:05:05.705 --> 00:05:08.790
shop, we have to
go to world space.

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:12.710
Now the nice thing with w being
zero is if you don't mind if

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.190
I grab that, as I translate

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:17.670
an object, the normal still

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:20.090
points, in this
case, straight up.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:21.590
So that's fine.

00:05:21.590 --> 00:05:23.090
That'll work.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:24.590
That'll switch off translations.

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:27.105
Cassidy can also rotate
things in the world.

00:05:27.105 --> 00:05:29.505
So you do want the
normal to rotate.

00:05:29.505 --> 00:05:31.825
So definitely a rotation matrix

00:05:31.825 --> 00:05:34.145
can be used to go from model

00:05:34.145 --> 00:05:36.560
space to world space.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.360
The tricky thing is scaling.

00:05:39.360 --> 00:05:43.360
If this object has a
uniform scale, you can imagine

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:45.480
that the normals just uniformly

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:47.600
go in the right direction.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:49.100
Just make sure
they're normalized.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:50.600
Have a length of one.

00:05:50.600 --> 00:05:52.365
The tricky thing is if you,

00:05:52.365 --> 00:05:54.765
like, skew this or flatten less.

00:05:54.765 --> 00:05:56.365
-Or a non-uniform scale?

00:05:56.365 --> 00:05:57.865
-Or a non-uniform scale.

00:05:57.865 --> 00:05:59.725
Imagine I just slam this down

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:01.885
and I turn it into
a flat surface.

00:06:01.885 --> 00:06:04.320
-What you'll find--
-They all have to go.

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:05.820
-Exactly. Yeah.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.320
Can you demonstrate that you

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:08.820
were doing something
over there?

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.600
-Well, if you flatten the ball,
that curve becomes flattened.

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:15.200
So the normals are
literally slowly flattening out

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.700
and pointing up more and more

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:18.200
as it flattens down
to the surface.

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.785
And the very edge ones are
slowly pointing out more.

00:06:20.785 --> 00:06:22.285
-Yeah.

00:06:22.285 --> 00:06:23.785
So that's something
we've got to be careful of.

00:06:23.785 --> 00:06:26.705
The non-uniform scale that's
why I was a bit scared when you

00:06:26.705 --> 00:06:29.745
wanted us to do a video
on skews and shears.

00:06:29.745 --> 00:06:31.245
I was like, oh, man.

00:06:31.245 --> 00:06:33.595
This is not.

00:06:33.595 --> 00:06:35.220
You don't even have a shear

00:06:35.220 --> 00:06:36.926
matrix in your math library.

00:06:36.926 --> 00:06:38.880
-On purpose.

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:40.380
But you can.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:41.880
You can.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:45.306
You can fake it by doing,
a scale with the three, right?

00:06:45.306 --> 00:06:48.405
The three values, you can just
make them non the same.

00:06:48.405 --> 00:06:50.325
And it will skew.

00:06:50.325 --> 00:06:51.825
-Skew the matrix.

00:06:51.825 --> 00:06:55.285
Yeah. See, I can literally show 
you a matrix skewing just by

00:06:55.285 --> 00:06:57.330
parenting two objects
together in the right order.

00:06:57.330 --> 00:07:00.610
And actually show you what
happens when you rotate one and

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:02.110
the object does this.

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:03.970
It's just

00:07:03.970 --> 00:07:05.490
happens because of the way--

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:08.050
-It inherits right first.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:09.650
-Yep. Just you'd have to...

00:07:11.025 --> 00:07:13.505
If you do it correctly,
it doesn't do it.

00:07:13.505 --> 00:07:15.985
But the idea being, yeah, if
you scale things, you can cause

00:07:15.985 --> 00:07:17.485
that problem.

00:07:17.485 --> 00:07:19.025
If you non-uniformly scale
things and parent them together

00:07:19.025 --> 00:07:20.785
and whatnot, then you can
run with that problem.

00:07:20.785 --> 00:07:22.625
-So the way we get out of this

00:07:22.625 --> 00:07:25.597
bind, it's called the
inverse transpose.

00:07:25.597 --> 00:07:28.240
Yeah. It's a bit of

00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:30.280
jargon, but the idea, 
just like

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:34.040
Paul was saying, these normals
on the side of this ball, as

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:36.440
the ball gets flattened out,
you want these normals to

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.600
actually start pointing up.

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.855
So as the ball gets as the
vertices get stretched to the

00:07:41.855 --> 00:07:44.775
right, you want this normal to

00:07:44.775 --> 00:07:47.695
point more to my left so that

00:07:47.695 --> 00:07:50.175
they all in the end, you want
them all pointing straight up.

00:07:50.175 --> 00:07:51.675
So, yeah, there's it's a move.

00:07:51.675 --> 00:07:55.215
It's called the inverse
transpose, and then we hack

00:07:55.215 --> 00:07:57.510
the matrix down into
a three by three.

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.270
There's some cool 
moves we do.

00:07:59.270 --> 00:08:00.758
-Yeah. It's easy.

00:08:00.758 --> 00:08:02.957
-There you go. Easy.

00:08:02.957 --> 00:08:06.770
-Yeah. Well, see, the rotations
are all orthogonal moves.

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:10.390
So a rotation along the x, the
y, and the z are orthogonal,

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:13.735
whereas your asymmetric scale
is not, and the translation is

00:08:13.735 --> 00:08:15.235
not orthogonal.

00:08:15.235 --> 00:08:17.895
Now, if you take the inverse
of a matrix, the inverse of an

00:08:17.895 --> 00:08:21.095
orthogonal matrix can also
be done by the transpose.

00:08:21.095 --> 00:08:24.935
So if we take the inverse of
the matrix, it'll only inverse

00:08:24.935 --> 00:08:26.620
the orthogonal parts.

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:28.120
And then when you take the

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:29.620
transpose, that's
also the inverse.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:31.340
We've taken the inverse of the
inverse, you come back to the

00:08:31.340 --> 00:08:32.840
same matrix you started with.

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:34.340
See what I mean?

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:35.840
It's easy.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:37.880
-Oh, yeah.

00:08:37.880 --> 00:08:40.313
I was thinking about that
over breakfast this morning.

00:08:40.313 --> 00:08:42.074
-And the only parts that get
thrown away are the parts that

00:08:42.074 --> 00:08:43.556
are not orthogonal.

00:08:43.556 --> 00:08:45.380
-My one problem with all

00:08:45.380 --> 00:08:46.880
this is what is orthogonal?

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:49.715
-It means that if I rotate
around the x, it's not messing

00:08:49.715 --> 00:08:52.515
with the y, and I'm
not messing with the z.

00:08:52.515 --> 00:08:54.960
So they are decoupled
from each other.

00:08:54.960 --> 00:08:57.520
And so they're all at
right angles to each other.

00:08:57.520 --> 00:09:01.040
-Yeah. They're like
this as opposed to yeah.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:03.100
That right?

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:04.880
These two vectors are
orthogonal to each other.

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:06.460
You know?

00:09:06.460 --> 00:09:08.160
You do that, and they're no
longer you're skewing the

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:09.660
matrix as you do this.

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:11.160
-Right.

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:12.660
-You know, an easier way to do
this, though, is just to write

00:09:12.660 --> 00:09:14.855
down the rotations that you're
doing, and don't write down the

00:09:14.855 --> 00:09:16.375
scales and the translates.

00:09:16.375 --> 00:09:18.295
And now I can rotate
to normals just plain.

00:09:18.295 --> 00:09:19.795
You don't have to get all fancy.

00:09:19.795 --> 00:09:22.050
-Makes my life a bit easier.

00:09:22.050 --> 00:09:23.550
Yeah. That's true.

00:09:23.550 --> 00:09:26.130
I'm going to move this
vector out the way, and then

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:29.570
Scott is going to talk 
to us about lighting.

00:09:29.570 --> 00:09:31.890
Okay. So we have a torch here.

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:33.390
Oh, yeah.

00:09:33.390 --> 00:09:36.005
I see some
lighting and a bolt.

00:09:36.005 --> 00:09:37.505
Yes. Go ahead.

00:09:37.505 --> 00:09:39.005
What's going on here?

00:09:39.005 --> 00:09:41.285
-The way I'm going to
mathematically pretend that

00:09:41.285 --> 00:09:44.363
there's a light source out here
is first off,

00:09:44.363 --> 00:09:46.385
get the light a position.

00:09:46.385 --> 00:09:48.620
And hold it
still for a second.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:52.300
And then I'm going to take the
property of a material like

00:09:52.300 --> 00:09:54.460
this, where the light will,

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:56.620
as the light comes down and

00:09:56.620 --> 00:09:58.260
strikes the surface, 
it's going to

00:09:58.260 --> 00:09:59.900
scatter off the surface in all

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:01.515
different directions.

00:10:01.515 --> 00:10:04.555
The guy that first made the
math model of this is a fellow

00:10:04.555 --> 00:10:07.915
named Phong and a lot of our
lighting models are based on

00:10:07.915 --> 00:10:09.415
his original work.

00:10:09.415 --> 00:10:10.915
All right.

00:10:10.915 --> 00:10:13.275
So light will scatter and the
rougher the surface, the more

00:10:13.275 --> 00:10:16.050
the light scatters about.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:19.650
So now the way to calculate
this is I need to find out

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:21.970
where your light source is.

00:10:21.970 --> 00:10:24.530
And then if I know the normal,
maybe if you can move the light

00:10:24.530 --> 00:10:26.930
source up to,
yeah, there you go.

00:10:26.930 --> 00:10:30.285
So if I move the light source
up to this normal, I can

00:10:30.285 --> 00:10:33.485
calculate a ray from
here down to here.

00:10:33.485 --> 00:10:36.685
The brightness, 
notice that the

00:10:36.685 --> 00:10:39.885
brightness fades 
away as you go

00:10:39.885 --> 00:10:42.040
away from the light source.

00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:45.640
And so what Phong noticed was if
the light source, now bring the

00:10:45.640 --> 00:10:49.160
light source directly on top
or nearly on top, that that is

00:10:49.160 --> 00:10:52.360
right here at the normal or
at that point, that is the

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:54.600
brightest point that
the light is going to be.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:56.280
And then as you fade down to

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:58.395
here, it becomes
less, less bright.

00:10:58.395 --> 00:10:59.895
You see that?

00:10:59.895 --> 00:11:01.395
-So if the normal is pointing

00:11:01.395 --> 00:11:03.195
directly back 
at the light source.

00:11:03.195 --> 00:11:06.955
-Now, what you also found or
noticed was that the rate at

00:11:06.955 --> 00:11:10.560
which it drops off can be done
with a trigonometric function

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.060
like a cosine.

00:11:12.060 --> 00:11:15.840
And so if I take the dot
product of the light position

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.960
with the normals
 to the surface,

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:20.080
that dot product ends up being

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:22.640
the cosine of the
angle between them.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:26.605
So if the angle between them
is zero, so it's straight down.

00:11:26.605 --> 00:11:29.165
We'll make that
maximum brightness.

00:11:29.165 --> 00:11:32.845
And then depending on what the
angle is down to here, it'll

00:11:32.845 --> 00:11:35.885
become less and less and less
as it drops off towards zero.

00:11:35.885 --> 00:11:37.907
-Right. So if my light is at

00:11:37.907 --> 00:11:39.930
90 degrees?

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:41.450
-Yeah. It's at 90 degrees.

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:42.950
It's not being lit.

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:46.250
And then, of course, the
backside of the ball is not

00:11:46.250 --> 00:11:49.930
being lit at all because
these are now all into the

00:11:49.930 --> 00:11:51.690
negative numbers with
the cosine function.

00:11:51.690 --> 00:11:54.175
-Makes sense because the dot
product, those two numbers,

00:11:54.175 --> 00:11:56.655
returns zero when they're
90 degrees to each

00:11:56.655 --> 00:11:59.015
other, the two vectors.

00:11:59.015 --> 00:12:01.375
-To make 
another visual demonstration,

00:12:01.375 --> 00:12:04.095
Cassidy has set up some
lights in the coffee shop.

00:12:04.095 --> 00:12:07.150
So maybe you could explain as
Cassidy is moving the light.

00:12:07.150 --> 00:12:10.430
-So as she moves the light back
and forth, it's just changing

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:11.930
the location of the light.

00:12:11.930 --> 00:12:14.830
The normals are coming up off
the surface of the object here,

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:17.470
and you can just see the
rate at which see this back

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:18.970
area back here?

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:21.825
If Cassidy moves the light back
and forth a little bit, see how

00:12:21.825 --> 00:12:23.325
it starts to get lit?

00:12:23.325 --> 00:12:25.985
And then now it's in,
essentially it's in shadow.

00:12:25.985 --> 00:12:29.105
So another part of that
equation is to start off by

00:12:29.105 --> 00:12:32.545
drawing the entire object in
what's called ambient light.

00:12:32.545 --> 00:12:35.600
So that's just the
light around us.

00:12:35.600 --> 00:12:37.100
I don't know.

00:12:37.100 --> 00:12:38.600
It's coming in from the
window, bouncing off walls.

00:12:38.600 --> 00:12:41.920
If you were in a if you were
on the moon where there's very

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:44.240
little ambient light,

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:46.880
this would be in complete
shadow and the shadowy area

00:12:46.880 --> 00:12:48.380
here would be black.

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:49.517
But around here, there's always

00:12:49.517 --> 00:12:50.655
going to be a little 
bit of light.

00:12:50.655 --> 00:12:53.575
So we draw the whole scene in

00:12:53.575 --> 00:12:56.495
ambient light, then draw it

00:12:56.495 --> 00:12:58.095
with the diffuse light.

00:12:58.095 --> 00:13:01.300
That's the light that scatters
off based on the direction.

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:03.780
And then I can make
one more pass at it.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:05.280
-That'll be this one, right?

00:13:05.280 --> 00:13:06.780
The diffuse light?

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:08.394
-That's the diffuse light.

00:13:08.394 --> 00:13:11.280
And then I can make one more
pass at it, which is that if

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:13.300
the light goes from here,
strikes the normal of the

00:13:13.300 --> 00:13:15.347
surface and then reflects

00:13:15.347 --> 00:13:17.395
and bounces into my eye,

00:13:17.395 --> 00:13:19.235
it looks like
the shiny spot.

00:13:19.235 --> 00:13:21.555
-So you can actually see it
lighting the bottom of my hand.

00:13:21.555 --> 00:13:23.376
Right?

00:13:23.376 --> 00:13:26.055
It's actually bouncing
off the light in the bottom of

00:13:26.055 --> 00:13:27.555
my hand now because it's
bouncing off the ball.

00:13:27.555 --> 00:13:29.055
-I do it a lot of times
with just my hand.

00:13:29.055 --> 00:13:30.755
If you put your hand out,
put the light above my hand.

00:13:30.755 --> 00:13:33.232
If you just have it now watch

00:13:33.232 --> 00:13:35.710
what happens to the colour or

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:38.430
the brightness of
my hand as I rotate.

00:13:38.430 --> 00:13:41.550
And you can see it
suddenly go into shadows.

00:13:41.550 --> 00:13:44.905
And so it drops off
rather rapidly at the end.

00:13:44.905 --> 00:13:47.945
-And that's when the
dot product goes to zero.

00:13:47.945 --> 00:13:49.625
So the cosine of the angle.

00:13:49.625 --> 00:13:53.145
-Arccosine of the dot
product, two vectors gives you

00:13:53.145 --> 00:13:54.665
the angle, right?

00:13:54.665 --> 00:13:56.165
Arccosine?

00:13:56.165 --> 00:13:58.185
-Yes. 
-I'm impressed.

00:13:59.870 --> 00:14:01.710
Wow.

00:14:01.710 --> 00:14:03.210
-Love that.

00:14:03.210 --> 00:14:05.150
It's almost like
I've done it before.

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:06.910
-Well, I'll definitely
have that on the website.

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:08.830
Yeah. That's a great
formula to use.

00:14:08.830 --> 00:14:10.330
Okay.

00:14:10.330 --> 00:14:11.830
So we're going to talk
more about normals.

00:14:11.830 --> 00:14:14.345
Boy, you're going to be
messing around with normals all

00:14:14.345 --> 00:14:16.825
over the place, so I think
we're going to switch over.

00:14:16.825 --> 00:14:18.425
-Show the normals
practically in three

00:14:18.425 --> 00:14:19.925
d space.

00:14:19.925 --> 00:14:22.325
-Oh, wow. This will be great.
Thank you.